1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,600
About sixty percent.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,920
That is how much of America's daily

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,607
supply of IV fluids came from a

4
00:00:06,607 --> 00:00:08,927
single factory in the mountains of North

5
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:09,961
Carolina,

6
00:00:09,961 --> 00:00:12,491
according to the American Hospital

7
00:00:12,491 --> 00:00:13,935
Association.

8
00:00:14,183 --> 00:00:16,381
In September twenty twenty-four,

9
00:00:16,381 --> 00:00:19,423
Hurricane Helene flooded that factory.

10
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:20,372
Within days,

11
00:00:20,372 --> 00:00:22,522
hospitals across the country were told

12
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:24,672
they could only order a fraction of

13
00:00:24,672 --> 00:00:26,992
the fluids they normally used.

14
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,566
So how does the richest country in

15
00:00:29,566 --> 00:00:31,882
the world end up depending on one

16
00:00:31,882 --> 00:00:34,798
building for a bag of salt water?

17
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:36,920
By the end of this video,

18
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,288
you will know what happened inside that

19
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:40,211
factory,

20
00:00:40,211 --> 00:00:43,052
how hospitals and regulators kept patients

21
00:00:43,052 --> 00:00:43,738
supplied,

22
00:00:43,738 --> 00:00:46,579
and why the economics of cheap medicine

23
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:48,836
keep creating the same weak point.

24
00:00:54,483 --> 00:00:56,943
Start with the product.

25
00:00:56,943 --> 00:00:59,409
An IV bag is mostly sterile water

26
00:00:59,409 --> 00:01:01,803
with a little salt or sugar.

27
00:01:01,993 --> 00:01:04,923
Hospitals use it for almost everything.

28
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:06,625
To keep patients hydrated,

29
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:07,889
to deliver drugs,

30
00:01:07,889 --> 00:01:09,153
during surgery,

31
00:01:09,153 --> 00:01:11,253
in the emergency room.

32
00:01:11,393 --> 00:01:12,944
It is one of the most common

33
00:01:12,944 --> 00:01:14,475
things in any hospital,

34
00:01:14,475 --> 00:01:16,238
and one of the cheapest.

35
00:01:16,483 --> 00:01:18,700
The factory is called North Cove,

36
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:20,643
near the town of Marion.

37
00:01:20,713 --> 00:01:22,056
It belongs to Baxter,

38
00:01:22,056 --> 00:01:24,213
a large medical company.

39
00:01:24,463 --> 00:01:27,228
Baxter calls it its largest manufacturing

40
00:01:27,228 --> 00:01:29,048
facility in the world,

41
00:01:29,048 --> 00:01:31,122
with more than two thousand five hundred

42
00:01:31,122 --> 00:01:32,183
employees.

43
00:01:32,423 --> 00:01:34,278
And it calls it the largest maker

44
00:01:34,278 --> 00:01:36,544
of IV and dialysis solutions in the

45
00:01:36,544 --> 00:01:38,175
United States.

46
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:40,787
The numbers are enormous.

47
00:01:40,787 --> 00:01:42,336
According to Baxter,

48
00:01:42,336 --> 00:01:44,582
the site produces more than a million

49
00:01:44,582 --> 00:01:47,427
IV solutions every single day.

50
00:01:47,677 --> 00:01:50,212
The hospital association put its share at

51
00:01:50,212 --> 00:01:52,747
about sixty percent of the IV solutions

52
00:01:52,747 --> 00:01:55,037
used in the country each day.

53
00:01:55,297 --> 00:01:57,755
Baxter itself never gave a percentage,

54
00:01:57,755 --> 00:02:00,420
so treat that figure as an estimate.

55
00:02:00,683 --> 00:02:02,303
The building itself is huge.

56
00:02:02,303 --> 00:02:04,749
Baxter's fact sheet describes more than

57
00:02:04,749 --> 00:02:06,895
one point four million square feet,

58
00:02:06,895 --> 00:02:09,612
and more than five hundred million dollars

59
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:12,058
invested in the site since twenty

60
00:02:12,058 --> 00:02:13,473
seventeen.

61
00:02:13,613 --> 00:02:15,065
It is not a small workshop that

62
00:02:15,065 --> 00:02:16,573
happened to flood.

63
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:18,609
It is the heart of the system,

64
00:02:18,609 --> 00:02:20,508
built to be efficient.

65
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:23,360
And there are not many other makers.

66
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:24,956
One competitor,

67
00:02:24,956 --> 00:02:26,552
ICU Medical,

68
00:02:26,552 --> 00:02:28,986
says in its annual report that its

69
00:02:28,986 --> 00:02:31,690
IV solutions compete in the U.S.

70
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:33,150
with Baxter and B.

71
00:02:33,210 --> 00:02:34,570
Braun.

72
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:35,537
Three companies,

73
00:02:35,537 --> 00:02:36,918
and one of them with one giant

74
00:02:36,918 --> 00:02:37,293
plant,

75
00:02:37,293 --> 00:02:39,471
carrying a large part of the load.

76
00:02:39,717 --> 00:02:42,167
Let me draw the supply line.

77
00:02:42,237 --> 00:02:44,577
Here is one big factory.

78
00:02:44,577 --> 00:02:45,221
From it,

79
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:47,567
trucks go out to distributors.

80
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:49,056
From the distributors,

81
00:02:49,056 --> 00:02:51,167
to thousands of hospitals.

82
00:02:51,227 --> 00:02:52,174
Next to it,

83
00:02:52,174 --> 00:02:53,987
two smaller makers.

84
00:02:54,107 --> 00:02:55,727
Now notice the shape.

85
00:02:55,727 --> 00:02:57,797
Most of the arrows start in the

86
00:02:57,797 --> 00:02:58,915
same place.

87
00:02:59,150 --> 00:03:00,086
For years,

88
00:03:00,086 --> 00:03:02,300
that shape worked fine.

89
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:02,948
Then,

90
00:03:02,948 --> 00:03:05,162
at the end of September twenty

91
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:06,122
twenty-four,

92
00:03:06,122 --> 00:03:07,460
the rain came.

93
00:03:18,117 --> 00:03:21,017
Hurricane Helene hit the southeast United

94
00:03:21,017 --> 00:03:23,917
States and dumped enormous amounts of rain

95
00:03:23,917 --> 00:03:25,850
on the mountains of western North

96
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:27,417
Carolina.

97
00:03:27,587 --> 00:03:29,697
Rivers rose fast.

98
00:03:29,737 --> 00:03:30,776
At North Cove,

99
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:31,816
Baxter said,

100
00:03:31,816 --> 00:03:34,481
heavy rain and the storm surge triggered

101
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:35,787
a levee breach,

102
00:03:35,787 --> 00:03:38,519
and water got into the site.

103
00:03:38,783 --> 00:03:40,522
By September twenty-ninth,

104
00:03:40,522 --> 00:03:42,943
Baxter announced that the site was closed

105
00:03:42,943 --> 00:03:44,423
for production.

106
00:03:44,523 --> 00:03:46,476
The bridges that led to the factory

107
00:03:46,476 --> 00:03:48,053
were damaged too.

108
00:03:48,113 --> 00:03:49,778
So the problem was not only the

109
00:03:49,778 --> 00:03:50,933
machines.

110
00:03:50,983 --> 00:03:53,462
Workers and trucks could not easily reach

111
00:03:53,462 --> 00:03:54,805
the building.

112
00:03:55,050 --> 00:03:57,561
Making sterile fluids is not like making

113
00:03:57,561 --> 00:03:59,530
ordinary products.

114
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:02,652
Every production line has to be cleaned,

115
00:04:02,652 --> 00:04:05,160
checked and proven safe before a single

116
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:06,900
bag can be shipped.

117
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:09,766
A flooded line cannot simply be dried

118
00:04:09,766 --> 00:04:11,954
out and switched back on.

119
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:14,277
Let me draw the flood.

120
00:04:14,277 --> 00:04:15,777
Here is the river.

121
00:04:15,777 --> 00:04:17,564
Here is the levee that holds it

122
00:04:17,564 --> 00:04:18,367
back.

123
00:04:18,367 --> 00:04:20,677
Here is the factory behind it.

124
00:04:20,787 --> 00:04:22,277
The water rises.

125
00:04:22,277 --> 00:04:23,637
The levee breaks.

126
00:04:23,637 --> 00:04:25,611
And one break in one place cuts

127
00:04:25,611 --> 00:04:27,866
off a large share of a whole

128
00:04:27,866 --> 00:04:29,594
country's supply.

129
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:30,957
Within days,

130
00:04:30,957 --> 00:04:34,678
hospitals across America felt it.

131
00:04:40,317 --> 00:04:43,407
Baxter put its customers on allocation.

132
00:04:43,407 --> 00:04:45,408
That means each hospital could only order

133
00:04:45,408 --> 00:04:47,045
a set share of what it normally

134
00:04:47,045 --> 00:04:47,827
bought.

135
00:04:47,877 --> 00:04:48,484
At first,

136
00:04:48,484 --> 00:04:50,786
direct customers could get forty percent

137
00:04:50,786 --> 00:04:52,669
of their usual orders of the most

138
00:04:52,669 --> 00:04:53,897
needed fluids.

139
00:04:53,957 --> 00:04:55,014
For distributors,

140
00:04:55,014 --> 00:04:56,867
it was only ten percent.

141
00:04:57,067 --> 00:04:58,294
On October ninth,

142
00:04:58,294 --> 00:05:00,562
Baxter raised both to sixty percent,

143
00:05:00,562 --> 00:05:02,434
and set a goal of returning to

144
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:04,931
ninety or one hundred percent for certain

145
00:05:04,931 --> 00:05:07,195
products by the end of the year.

146
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:09,992
Hospital pharmacists described the

147
00:05:09,992 --> 00:05:11,100
pressure.

148
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:14,364
In a survey by their professional society

149
00:05:14,364 --> 00:05:15,511
in October,

150
00:05:15,511 --> 00:05:17,852
eighty-four percent of respondents said

151
00:05:17,852 --> 00:05:20,662
the shortage had a moderate or critical

152
00:05:20,662 --> 00:05:21,730
impact.

153
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:24,495
A critical impact meant delaying or

154
00:05:24,495 --> 00:05:27,640
canceling treatments or procedures.

155
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:30,426
And almost half of those who manage

156
00:05:30,426 --> 00:05:32,742
supplies said they had two weeks of

157
00:05:32,742 --> 00:05:34,211
fluids or less.

158
00:05:34,450 --> 00:05:37,480
Regulators tried to stretch the supply.

159
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:40,189
The FDA issued temporary guidance that let

160
00:05:40,189 --> 00:05:42,153
pharmacies prepare certain IV fluids

161
00:05:42,153 --> 00:05:43,004
themselves,

162
00:05:43,004 --> 00:05:45,405
something they normally could not do on

163
00:05:45,405 --> 00:05:46,660
this scale.

164
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:47,998
Pharmacists,

165
00:05:47,998 --> 00:05:50,457
doctors and nurses had to decide,

166
00:05:50,457 --> 00:05:51,922
patient by patient,

167
00:05:51,922 --> 00:05:54,260
where each bag mattered most.

168
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:54,900
For weeks,

169
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:57,052
every single bag had to count,

170
00:05:57,052 --> 00:05:59,877
from the emergency room to the operating

171
00:05:59,877 --> 00:06:00,694
room.

172
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:03,480
Let me draw the allocation.

173
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:05,575
Here is a normal order,

174
00:06:05,575 --> 00:06:07,150
one hundred bags.

175
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:08,572
Under allocation,

176
00:06:08,572 --> 00:06:11,000
a direct customer gets sixty.

177
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,240
A distributor,

178
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:12,849
at first,

179
00:06:12,849 --> 00:06:14,290
got only ten.

180
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:16,485
The gap between the order and the

181
00:06:16,485 --> 00:06:18,960
delivery is where hospitals had to

182
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:20,235
improvise.

183
00:06:20,483 --> 00:06:23,693
So the question became simple.

184
00:06:23,793 --> 00:06:26,969
Where could America find millions of extra

185
00:06:26,969 --> 00:06:27,472
bags,

186
00:06:27,472 --> 00:06:28,337
fast?

187
00:06:33,983 --> 00:06:36,533
Regulators moved first.

188
00:06:36,643 --> 00:06:39,019
The Food and Drug Administration added

189
00:06:39,019 --> 00:06:41,827
several IV and dialysis solutions to its

190
00:06:41,827 --> 00:06:43,723
official shortage list.

191
00:06:44,023 --> 00:06:47,209
And it helped clear the temporary import

192
00:06:47,209 --> 00:06:50,395
of twenty-three different IV and dialysis

193
00:06:50,395 --> 00:06:53,051
fluids from five Baxter factories around

194
00:06:53,051 --> 00:06:54,182
the world.

195
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:56,297
Then came the planes.

196
00:06:56,297 --> 00:06:57,835
According to Baxter,

197
00:06:57,835 --> 00:07:00,042
by the end of twenty twenty-four,

198
00:07:00,042 --> 00:07:02,940
about two hundred airplanes had delivered

199
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:05,147
product to the United States,

200
00:07:05,147 --> 00:07:07,354
nearly eighteen thousand tons of fluids,

201
00:07:07,354 --> 00:07:09,577
from plants in its global network.

202
00:07:09,577 --> 00:07:10,330
Water,

203
00:07:10,330 --> 00:07:11,863
flown across oceans,

204
00:07:11,863 --> 00:07:15,320
because the usual source was under repair.

205
00:07:15,583 --> 00:07:17,883
The other makers pushed too.

206
00:07:17,973 --> 00:07:21,523
B.

207
00:07:21,523 --> 00:07:22,473
Braun said its plants in California and

208
00:07:22,473 --> 00:07:23,423
Florida would raise output by twenty

209
00:07:23,423 --> 00:07:24,213
percent.

210
00:07:24,563 --> 00:07:27,591
ICU Medical reported higher demand in the

211
00:07:27,591 --> 00:07:29,273
last months of the year.

212
00:07:29,563 --> 00:07:31,702
But neither could replace a plant of

213
00:07:31,702 --> 00:07:33,491
that size overnight.

214
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:35,298
Inside North Cove,

215
00:07:35,298 --> 00:07:38,090
the repair went line by line.

216
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:40,782
Baxter restarted its first line about four

217
00:07:40,782 --> 00:07:42,960
and a half weeks after the flood,

218
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,407
and released its first bags made after

219
00:07:45,407 --> 00:07:48,210
the storm on November twenty-first.

220
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:49,685
It was the biggest one,

221
00:07:49,685 --> 00:07:52,430
about a quarter of the site's output.

222
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:54,978
By late January twenty twenty-five,

223
00:07:54,978 --> 00:07:57,550
all ten lines were running again.

224
00:07:57,690 --> 00:07:59,484
On February seventeenth,

225
00:07:59,484 --> 00:08:01,301
Baxter said the site was back to

226
00:08:01,301 --> 00:08:03,573
its production levels from before the

227
00:08:03,573 --> 00:08:04,400
storm.

228
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,417
That was less than five months after

229
00:08:06,417 --> 00:08:07,502
the flood.

230
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:10,510
The recovery was expensive.

231
00:08:10,730 --> 00:08:12,424
In its annual report,

232
00:08:12,424 --> 00:08:15,124
Baxter estimated that the storm cut its

233
00:08:15,124 --> 00:08:17,579
sales by one hundred and ten million

234
00:08:17,579 --> 00:08:18,670
dollars.

235
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:21,552
It recorded three hundred and seventeen

236
00:08:21,552 --> 00:08:23,600
million dollars of cleanup,

237
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,648
idle-plant and air freight costs,

238
00:08:25,648 --> 00:08:27,982
offset by two hundred and fifty-one

239
00:08:27,982 --> 00:08:30,656
million dollars from insurance.

240
00:08:30,917 --> 00:08:33,217
Let me draw the recovery.

241
00:08:33,497 --> 00:08:34,592
Here is the flood,

242
00:08:34,592 --> 00:08:36,757
at the end of September.

243
00:08:36,927 --> 00:08:37,758
The first line,

244
00:08:37,758 --> 00:08:39,637
about a month later.

245
00:08:39,667 --> 00:08:40,738
All ten lines,

246
00:08:40,738 --> 00:08:42,867
at the end of January.

247
00:08:42,867 --> 00:08:43,805
Full output,

248
00:08:43,805 --> 00:08:45,367
in February.

249
00:08:45,417 --> 00:08:47,576
And in May twenty twenty-five,

250
00:08:47,576 --> 00:08:51,087
almost every product off allocation.

251
00:08:51,187 --> 00:08:52,095
Eight months,

252
00:08:52,095 --> 00:08:54,261
from flood to normal.

253
00:08:54,517 --> 00:08:56,607
The fluids came back.

254
00:08:56,607 --> 00:08:58,489
But the bigger question did not go

255
00:08:58,489 --> 00:08:59,507
away.

256
00:08:59,717 --> 00:09:02,400
Why was one building carrying so much

257
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,192
in the first place?

258
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:12,470
The answer is in the price tag.

259
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:15,491
The FDA studied drug shortages and listed

260
00:09:15,491 --> 00:09:17,356
the first root cause as a lack

261
00:09:17,356 --> 00:09:20,554
of incentives to make less profitable

262
00:09:20,554 --> 00:09:21,420
drugs.

263
00:09:21,750 --> 00:09:23,843
Of the shortage drugs it studied,

264
00:09:23,843 --> 00:09:25,959
sixty-three percent were sterile

265
00:09:25,959 --> 00:09:27,500
injectables.

266
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:29,809
In the year before the shortage,

267
00:09:29,809 --> 00:09:31,941
the median price per unit was just

268
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:34,673
eight dollars and seventy-three cents.

269
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:36,847
A government watchdog,

270
00:09:36,847 --> 00:09:37,659
the GAO,

271
00:09:37,659 --> 00:09:40,497
had found the same thing years earlier.

272
00:09:40,637 --> 00:09:43,332
Low profit margins had limited investment

273
00:09:43,332 --> 00:09:45,578
in factories for these cheap sterile

274
00:09:45,578 --> 00:09:46,627
products.

275
00:09:46,787 --> 00:09:48,149
When margins are thin,

276
00:09:48,149 --> 00:09:50,227
the cheapest way to make a product

277
00:09:50,227 --> 00:09:51,589
is in one huge plant,

278
00:09:51,589 --> 00:09:54,360
not in several smaller ones spread across

279
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:55,653
the country.

280
00:09:55,917 --> 00:09:58,177
And it had happened before.

281
00:09:58,417 --> 00:09:59,845
In twenty seventeen,

282
00:09:59,845 --> 00:10:01,916
Hurricane Maria disrupted saline

283
00:10:01,916 --> 00:10:04,193
production at a Baxter plant in Puerto

284
00:10:04,193 --> 00:10:05,207
Rico.

285
00:10:05,257 --> 00:10:08,624
Saline was already in short supply,

286
00:10:08,624 --> 00:10:10,807
and the storm made it worse.

287
00:10:10,807 --> 00:10:11,352
Then too,

288
00:10:11,352 --> 00:10:13,233
regulators let in fluids from Baxter

289
00:10:13,233 --> 00:10:14,397
plants abroad.

290
00:10:14,477 --> 00:10:16,568
The FDA described it in its own

291
00:10:16,568 --> 00:10:19,125
annual report on drug shortages for that

292
00:10:19,125 --> 00:10:19,957
year.

293
00:10:20,247 --> 00:10:20,998
Two storms,

294
00:10:20,998 --> 00:10:22,527
seven years apart,

295
00:10:22,527 --> 00:10:24,858
and the same lesson about the same

296
00:10:24,858 --> 00:10:26,494
kind of product.

297
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:29,040
Let me draw the trade-off.

298
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:30,351
On one side,

299
00:10:30,351 --> 00:10:31,810
one big plant.

300
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:33,087
Cheaper per bag,

301
00:10:33,087 --> 00:10:34,730
but one weak point.

302
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:35,790
On the other,

303
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:37,210
many smaller plants.

304
00:10:37,210 --> 00:10:38,422
More expensive,

305
00:10:38,422 --> 00:10:39,634
but if one floods,

306
00:10:39,634 --> 00:10:41,470
the others keep going.

307
00:10:41,790 --> 00:10:43,695
For a product sold for a few

308
00:10:43,695 --> 00:10:44,386
dollars,

309
00:10:44,386 --> 00:10:46,291
the market keeps choosing the first

310
00:10:46,291 --> 00:10:47,367
option.

311
00:10:47,617 --> 00:10:50,617
So how did one flooded factory run

312
00:10:50,617 --> 00:10:53,617
America short of IV fluids?

313
00:10:53,707 --> 00:10:55,635
Because the product is so cheap that

314
00:10:55,635 --> 00:10:58,377
nobody is paid to build a backup.

315
00:10:58,377 --> 00:11:00,777
The system is efficient on a normal

316
00:11:00,777 --> 00:11:01,192
day,

317
00:11:01,192 --> 00:11:03,537
and fragile on a bad one.

318
00:11:03,727 --> 00:11:06,267
The bags came back within months.

319
00:11:06,427 --> 00:11:08,432
The shape of the supply chain,

320
00:11:08,432 --> 00:11:10,970
with so many arrows starting in one

321
00:11:10,970 --> 00:11:11,452
place,

322
00:11:11,452 --> 00:11:13,575
is still the part to watch.

323
00:11:13,817 --> 00:11:16,185
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

324
00:11:16,185 --> 00:11:19,009
we follow the money behind another hidden

325
00:11:19,009 --> 00:11:21,347
link of the world economy.

326
00:11:21,567 --> 00:11:23,841
This is The Hidden Economics.
